// TPR-07-020 topology pin: take-project inside a loop where the loop
// header is reachable through a back-edge from a bypass-safe latch.
//
// `compute_bypass_safe_entries` previously selected a bypass-safe
// block as a "region entry" only when it had no predecessors OR at
// least one non-bypass-safe predecessor. A loop header that is the
// function entry has its own latch (back-edge) as a predecessor, and
// if the entire loop body is bypass-safe for some take-project class,
// every predecessor is bypass-safe — so neither `preds.is_empty()`
// nor "any non-bypass pred" holds, and no entry block is selected.
// Source 1's per-class drop never fires anywhere for that reachable
// bypass-safe region while edge cleanup also skips in-class vars,
// producing a real leak.
//
// The TPR-07-020 fix in `compute_bypass_safe_entries` treats the
// function entry block as an implicit "outside caller" predecessor
// that is non-bypass-safe by definition. With this, a loop-headed
// function whose loop body is bypass-safe still gets its function
// entry block selected as a region root.
//
// This topology pin uses an explicit `loop { ... break }` construct
// that holds an unconsumed take-project source enum across the loop
// body. The loop body never reaches the take-project (bypass path),
// and the loop header is reached both from the function entry (the
// initial fall-through) and from any back-edge if present. After the
// loop exits, the source enum must still be dropped via the per-class
// scope-exit path.

type MaybeIter = Empty | Holds(it: Iterator<int>);

@main () -> int = {
    let x: MaybeIter = Holds(it: [1, 2, 3].iter());
    let do_take = false;
    let iters = 0;
    loop {
        iters = iters + 1;
        if iters >= 1 then
            break
    };
    let count = if do_take then
        match x {
            Empty -> 0,
            Holds(it) -> it.count()
        }
    else
        0;
    count + iters - 1
}
